{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists","id":1553442,"title":"Tracking Damage for Antagonists","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-08-25T11:19:29-07:00","id":1553442,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Tracking Damage for Antagonists","content":"My homebrew system uses a health box system just like chronicles does. It works exactly the same. I love about everything about the Bashing/Lethal/Aggravated system, except for one thing : It is such a chore to track on antagonists.<br /> <br /> Sure when the party is fighting a single enemy, it goes fairly well.<br /> <br /> Where I begin to struggle is when multiple adversaries are involved. There are some things I already do to speed things up :<ul><li>When an enemy takes more lethal damage than their stamina in one hit, I remove them from the fight.</li> <li>I don\u2019t let every adversary fight till the end, when they realise they are losing, they start to flee.</li> <li>I ''wing it'' with some weaker ennemies, they are either dead 1 one massive hit, or dead in two regular hit, but i kind of dislike doing this, there's too much risk of confusion, and some weaker ennemies still wear armor that would realistically make them survive a few hit.</li> </ul>It works well when only one type of damage is dealt, but when mulitple type of damage starts stacking up, it becomes a nightmare.<br /> <br /> For example, if a stamina 4 human(9 health box) sits at 5 lethal, 3 bashing, and they take 4 bashing, it means they suddenly find themselves at 8 Lethal, 1 Bashing.<br /> <br /> To me, this is not intuitive at all, I had to take a sheet of paper to graph it out. Its not that hard, but when running a fight with multiple players and multiple opponents, the time it takes to calculate damage can rack up easily.<br /> <br /> Currently, I use an excel sheet that stacks type of damage in a way that shows me when an NPC\u2019s health track is full of a certain type of damage and if that track has any \u2018\u2019over\u2019\u2019 that I need to convert, but it\u2019s fair from perfect.<br /> <br /> As anyone had that problem before?<br /> <br /> How do you folks handle it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-25T11:55:00-07:00","id":1553445,"title":"","content":"First, the &quot;Beaten Down&quot; rules for CoD 2nd Edition has it that humans flee/give up upon taking more than Stamina in <i>Bashing</i>, regardless of how many hits to get there, or <i>any level</i> of Lethal or Aggravated. I personally think it works fine most of the time.<br /> <br /> But really, I usually don't bother with damage type if it won't make a big difference.<br /> <br /> Most opponents will leave combat when full of damage regardless of type. Mortals can't function full of Bashing, others will still prefer to give up rather than start accruing Lethal. If they're not willing to die in this fight, then they'll probably give up as soon as they take half their Health in damage. If push comes to shove, almost everyone will still stop and fall when full of Lethal.<br /> <br /> In this take, in most battles damage type serves only to decide if the fallen opponents are just unconscious, dying or dead, and you only have to know the type of their last Health Box, so I make a judgement call on it without really calculating and call it a day. The other function is to track healing, but most of the time I won't track healing for NPCs.<br /> <br /> So I only track damage types in their Health Track fully when the dramatic needs of the scene calls for it. The PCs must pull their punches to not kill, they're on a hit-and-run tactic where tracking healing for their target is relevant, the opponent is resilient to their attacks and tracking rollover is the whole point, things like that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-25T12:57:53-07:00","id":1553448,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553445#post1553445\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First, the &quot;Beaten Down&quot; rules for CoD 2nd Edition has it that humans flee/give up upon taking more than Stamina in <i>Bashing</i>, regardless of how many hits to get there, or <i>any level</i> of Lethal or Aggravated. I personally think it works fine most of the time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that works very well in a game in which violence is rare and dramatic. My current homebrew campaign is a 1920 Fantasy Adventure set in a fantasy world, so violence is rather frequent and both players and more than enemies they are fighting are expecting to fight to the death.<br /> <br /> My system also uses willpower as a \u2018\u2019stress health bar\u2019\u2019 , to handle things like being beaten down, but I don\u2019t want to get lost in the details too much. Functionally for damage tracking, my system works exactly like CoD.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553445#post1553445\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> So I only track damage types in their Health Track fully when the dramatic needs of the scene calls for it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah normally this is what I would do, but my campaign features quite a bit of combat.<br /> <br /> I did make some fast battle rules to help dealing with battles that are not narratively interesting but it doesn\u2019t always save me.<br /> <br /> Maybe I just shot myself in the foot when designing my system by taking chronicles health box system instead of a simpler one. My rules for healing and health box are much harsher than chronicles for mundane humans, but I really love how the basic health box system works for player characters.<br /> <br /> Maybe I\u2019ll just have to get better using my new excel sheet tracker, which I\u2019ll have to test next session.<br /> <br /> Thanks for the input! \ud83d\ude0a<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-25T16:36:36-07:00","id":1553450,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553448#post1553448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, that works very well in a game in which violence is rare and dramatic.</div> </div> </div> </div>I use similar principles in D&amp;D, it has nothing to do with the frequency of combat or being high fantasy. My D&amp;D monsters still want to live, so they won't fight to the death if they can avoid it, even animals and most other monsters living or undead.<br /> <br /> What I do, if I need to (in D&amp;D) is to adjust Hit Points as needed to have combat as hard or easy as I want, but almost every monster will still give up and flee if they're loosing.<br /> <br /> And I do use the distinction between Bashing and Lethal in D&amp;D 5th edition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553448#post1553448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah normally this is what I would do, but my campaign features quite a bit of combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>IME even with frequent combats, this distinction is still irrelevant most of the time. What matters isn't when NPCs die, it's when they stop fighting.<br /> <br /> Unless you're using regular healing effects for the NPCs that distinguish between damage types <b>and</b> can bring them from a fall back to the fight, I'm still failing to see the problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553448#post1553448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe I just shot myself in the foot when designing my system by taking chronicles health box system instead of a simpler one.</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably, if your main intent is to have traditional fantasy battles that are regular and end with death most of the time.<br /> <br /> While I also like it a lot, CoD's system wasn't designed for that. It can be used like that no problem IMO, but you would probably be better served with a simpler system.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553448#post1553448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">but I really love how the basic health box system works for player characters.</div> </div> </div> </div>PCs aren't NPCs.<br /> <br /> While I understand the appeal, I really do, but you shouldn't be so attached to the idea of using the exact same rules for PCs and NPCs.<br /> <br /> I have a couple suggestions that may interest you. Choose one if you like it.<br /> <br /> 1- Simplify the rules for NPCs, they just track damage. If they should be able to fight full of Bashing, double their Health Boxes. If they should be able to fight full of Lethal, triple their Health Boxes. When PCs deal damage to enemies, double Lethal if they have double Health Boxes, triple Aggravated if they have triple Health Boxes. You'll have something sufficiently similar to how the system works, but will only have to track damage straight;<br /> <br /> 2- Simplify one rule for everyone: remove roll over damage. If a character is full of Bashing, the next Bashing damage becomes Lethal. If a character is full of Lethal, the next damage becomes Aggravated. But the attack that fills their Health Bar discards any excess damage, so if a character has 2 unfilled Health Boxes and takes 4 Bashing, they only take 2 Bashing and discard the other two.<br /> <br /> Option 1 makes for a result that may take some time to get used to, but is mathematically very similar to the current system. The enemy will require basically the same amount of hits to defeat they would require, however you combine this damage.<br /> <br /> You really change how the game plays out a little with option 2, but not necessarily in a bad way. It changes the strategy around the decision over which type of damage to apply and slightly increases the advantage of causing a &quot;better&quot; type of damage. It also guarantees that using an inferior type to the one required by the enemy won't ever 1-hit kill them, which in a combat-heavy strategic kind of game is a win in my book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-26T08:48:11-07:00","id":1553459,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553450#post1553450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I use similar principles in D&amp;D, it has nothing to do with the frequency of combat or being high fantasy. My D&amp;D monsters still want to live, so they won't fight to the death if they can avoid it, even animals and most other monsters living or undead.</div> </div> </div> </div>I might not have expressed myself properly. I was strictly referring to the beaten down rules, which is a bit <i>harsh</i>, in my opinion, for a fantasy-style campaign. I do make enemies attempt to flee all the time, but I don\u2019t make them flee at the first point of lethal damage. The Beaten Down rule does allow a character to spend willpower to keep fighting, but that would be way too much bookkeeping for simple NPCs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553450#post1553450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I do use the distinction between Bashing and Lethal in D&amp;D 5th edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh? That sounds interesting. How does it work?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553450#post1553450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">IME even with frequent combats, this distinction is still irrelevant most of the time. What matters isn't when NPCs die, it's when they stop fighting.<br /> <br /> Unless you're using regular healing effects for the NPCs that distinguish between damage types and can bring them from a fall back to the fight, I'm still failing to see the problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do as a matter of a fact use them from time to time. Some monsters can leech life, others have access to mages and priests that can heal wounds, etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553450#post1553450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Probably, if your main intent is to have traditional fantasy battles that are regular and end with death most of the time.<br /> <br /> While I also like it a lot, CoD's system wasn't designed for that. It can be used like that no problem IMO, but you would probably be better served with a simpler system.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's true, which is why I already make use of a lot of short cuts, as mentionned in my opening post. Regular mortels tends not to be that much of a problem, as they tend to get massive damage (more than stamina) in one hit. It becomes more of a problem when fighting bigger or tougher ennemies.<br /> <br /> What I think my system handle well is the severity of the battle and its aftermath. I also like how it plays in general. I really just need to find a way to speed damage resolution on my head.<br /> <br /> If I can give a pitch meeting as simple as possible :<br /> <br /> It\u2019s a point buy System that takes heavy inspiration from CoD and some inspirations from DnD, Savage\u2019s world, Shadowrun, and Gurps.<br /> <br /> <b>CoD : </b>The attribute and skill system is all CoD. Its die resolution system is d10 based, each 6+ are successes, dices explode on a 10 and difficulties are scaled a bit like V5.<br /> <br /> <b>DnD : </b>Combat is somewhat inspired from DnD in the sense it that it plays well on a tabletop map with miniatures and characters each has an Action a Minor action and a reaction. There are rules for the attack of opportunities, etc. I also made some rules for rare \u2018\u2019legendary monsters\u2019\u2019 taking inspiration from DnD and monster hunter to handle boss fight, those are the main exception to ''NPC and PC'' mechanical dissonance.<br /> <br /> <b>Savage Word &amp; Shadowrun :</b> My magic system is inspired by Savage World in the sense that spells are generic and are flavoured with \u2018\u2019theme that only comes into play when relevant. It\u2019s inspired by Shadowrun by having spells cost deal mitigable damage to a resource, so mage could potentially cast a lot of weak spells without getting tired, or cast one big spell and go out of juice.<br /> <br /> <b>Gurps : </b>While my merits are closer to CoD in design, I did take some inspiration from some Gurps advantages, and drawbacks. My merit system can also handle PCs that are a lot more varied than basic CoD, without resorting to splats.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I\u2019ll stop myself here I get too enthusiastic talking about my project. So far I really like how it plays, just need to fix the damage calculation resolution.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553450#post1553450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> PCs aren't NPCs.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is very true. That's also an advice I tend to give a lot.<br /> <br /> However, I did not design my current system with a lot of mechanical dissonance for PC and NPC in mind. This would require changing a lot of spells to work differently, which I\u2019d rather not do at the moment, as I\u2019m mid campaign.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553450#post1553450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> 1- Simplify the rules for NPCs, they just track damage. If they should be able to fight full of Bashing, double their Health Boxes. If they should be able to fight full of lethal, triple their Health Boxes. When PCs deal damage to enemies, double Lethal if they have double Health Boxes, triple Aggravated if they have triple Health Boxes. You'll have something sufficiently similar to how the system works, but will only have to track damage straight;</div> </div> </div> </div>That idea floated in my mind at once, this is a very good suggestion, thank you.<br /> <br /> I think it works very well, but it\u2019s not without issues.<br /> <br /> If bashing deal 1, lethal deal 2 and aggravated deal 3, it works on a fresh enemy, but once the full track has been filled, lethal and aggravated starts doing too much damage.<br /> <br /> For example, let\u2019s take a mindless zombie that fights till its knocked out, full of lethal damage. If it has 8 health boxes, it means it would have 16 hp. As soon as it reaches 8 hp or less, then lethal should stop dealing double damage and aggravated should deal 1 less point of damage.<br /> <br /> Aggravated damage might even need to be lowered to 2 damage from the get-go if the Hit points reflect taken out of the combat at full lethal, as they aren\u2019t really that much more meaningful than lethal until the health track is full.<br /> <br /> It can also mess up healing a bit, but that\u2019s fine, i could likewise design ennemy healing to take this into account, by having health fractions represent different state of damage. Maybe an ennemy at lower than half hp can only receive ''lethal healing'' instead of bashing.<br /> <br /> Overall still a good middle ground idea that I might end up using if I can\u2019t manage with my Excel sheet. I\u2019ll truly know next Wednesday.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553450#post1553450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Simplify one rule for everyone: remove roll over damage. If a character is full of Bashing, the next Bashing damage becomes Lethal. If a character is full of Lethal, the next damage becomes Aggravated. But the attack that fills their Health Bar discards any excess damage, so if a character has 2 unfilled Health Boxes and takes 4 Bashing, they only take 2 Bashing and discard the other two.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is interesting and I think you made some good point for it, but even outside of some problem it might cause with some of my existing rule, I foresee a frustrating design : It risk nearly nullifying some players turn.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s imagine the following scenario :<ul><li>A player scores 5 successes on an attack, they have a claymore adding 4 damage and even use an ability to boost their damage by 2.</li> <li>the enemy has their 9 health boxes sitting at 8 bashing and they have a special resistance to a weapon that downgrades damage from lethal to bashing.</li> </ul><br /> With option 2 not only do they find themselves at 9 bashing instead of 1 Agg and 8 lethal, but that player not only wasted a good roll but also wasted resources on an ability for absolutely no results.<br /> <br /> This is an extreme example, but i can foresee it happening more than once.<br /> <br /> Still, thanks for the suggestions, I\u2019ll keep them in mind! \ud83d\ude0a<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-26T11:18:24-07:00","id":1553464,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553459#post1553459\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh? That sounds interesting. How does it work?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's a 3rd edition rule. It's very similar to CoD's types, but without rollout.<br /> <br /> While Lethal damage reduces current Hit Points, Bashing damage accumulates as a separate number. If Bashing = Current HP, the character is out cold, but stable, and will recover consciousness in some time.<br /> <br /> Bashing heals in a matter of minutes for each point, so it's very similar indeed to CoD.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553459#post1553459\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I can give a pitch meeting as simple as possible :</div> </div> </div> </div>Whenever you have a document for this, I think it would be interesting to read.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553459#post1553459\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If bashing deal 1, lethal deal 2 and aggravated deal 3, it works on a fresh enemy, but once the full track has been filled, lethal and aggravated starts doing too much damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>Keeping in mind that the idea is to only increase their output if the target has increased Health Boxes. A zombie with 8 HB that falls full of Lethal becomes 16 HB and takes double damage from Lethal <b>and Aggravated</b>, Agg isn't tripled for it. I wouldn't really reduce this damage as they lose Health Boxes, I don't think the mathematical difference is worth it. Reducing damage for Lethal is only relevant if the enemy has half or more HB filled with Bashing, I wouldn't care too much about it.<br /> <br /> The bigger &quot;problem&quot; is with how Healing works, but then just because it makes every heal to matter. Other than that, it simply applies the same rationale: if Lethal causes double, it also heals double, if Agg deals triple, it also heals triple.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553459#post1553459\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With option 2 not only do they find themselves at 9 bashing instead of 1 Agg and 8 lethal, but that player not only wasted a good roll but also wasted resources on an ability for absolutely no results.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's why I said it changes strategies.<br /> <br /> \u200bIn practice the player, knowing it won't roll over, will make different decisions. They'll either chose an attack that's easier to hit, or an attack that certainly causes Lethal or Agg. And the monster lasts longer (which is kind of the idea for a monster that only stops at full Agg).<br /> <br /> So this will certainly happen, but it isn't necessarily a bad thing. What it does is to change how the game plays and which strategies are best at any given time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-27T05:52:00-07:00","id":1553478,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553464#post1553464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's a 3rd edition rule. It's very similar to CoD's types, but without rollout.<br /> <br /> While Lethal damage reduces current Hit Points, Bashing damage accumulates as a separate number. If Bashing = Current HP, the character is out cold, but stable, and will recover consciousness in some time.<br /> <br /> Bashing heals in a matter of minutes for each point, so it's very similar indeed to CoD.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah yes, I remember that rule from 3.5!<br /> <br /> I remember not using it that much back in the days as either I was playing wizards and it\u2019s hard to deal non-lethal damage with spells or the -4 penalty to attack felt too hefty for the benefits. Still, we did use it when subduing enemies was the goal, even though we often dealt lethal damage and waited for them to be hurt before knocking them down with the last one or two hit in non-lethal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553464#post1553464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Whenever you have a document for this, I think it would be interesting to read.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course, I\u2019d be delighted to share it! I do have some documents, my main one, which handles character options and most rules is roughly 190 pages long, or 100k-ish words and I have some side pages on how I handle a group of enemies and legendary monsters and individual pages for my bestiary monsters&amp;NPC as i print them.<br /> <br /> Disclaimer: it is a bit of a mess right now. I like to think it\u2019s readable, but there\u2019s definitely a lot of grammar errors, probably some weird sentences left in there and some of my jokes might not completely land; I do use sarcasm or a humorous tone in some descriptions.<br /> <br /> It does have reference and it is structured, but some old rules I need to revise and I already plan on making some changes, as soon as my current campaign is over.<br /> <br /> I can try to have it ready as soon as possible. Might be a few days, or maybe a few weeks, I can\u2019t promise anything.<br /> <br /> But I\u2019d definitely be happy to share it!<br /> <br /> Can I share a word document on the forum?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553464#post1553464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Keeping in mind that the idea is to only increase their output if the target has increased Health Boxes. A zombie with 8 HB that falls full of Lethal becomes 16 HB and takes double damage from Lethal <b>and Aggravated</b>, Agg isn't tripled for it. I wouldn't really reduce this damage as they lose Health Boxes, I don't think the mathematical difference is worth it. Reducing damage for Lethal is only relevant if the enemy has half or more HB filled with Bashing, I wouldn't care too much about it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that makes more sense. It does render some of my aggravated option a lot more niche, but at the same time, it kinda of currently is. Aggravated has always only about being harder to heal and scarier, but it doesn\u2019t really have that much more impact than lethal damage does.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553464#post1553464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> The bigger &quot;problem&quot; is with how Healing works, but then just because it makes every heal to matter. Other than that, it simply applies the same rationale: if Lethal causes double, it also heals double, if Agg deals triple, it also heals triple.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, this also makes sense. Certain types of healing could also be blocked at certain threshold. For example, if an enemy can only heal bashing damage, maybe they can no longer heal once they are past half their hit points.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1553442-tracking-damage-for-antagonists?p=1553464#post1553464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's why I said it changes strategies.<br /> <br /> In practice the player, knowing it won't roll over, will make different decisions. They'll either chose an attack that's easier to hit, or an attack that certainly causes Lethal or Agg. And the monster lasts longer (which is kind of the idea for a monster that only stops at full Agg).<br /> <br /> So this will certainly happen, but it isn't necessarily a bad thing. What it does is to change how the game plays and which strategies are best at any given time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, it\u2019s interesting and definitely change the dynamic, i juste anticipated the kind of frustration it could cause. I already have one or two players on mind, lol.<br /> <br /> I think it would work well for boss enemies with multiple phases, which my legendary monster\u2019s kind of have with getting new moves and increased legendary actions as they get more hurt.<br /> <br /> I wouldn\u2019t use it for all enemies, however.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}